<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-frostbite neuropathic pain of the fingers, improving on duloxetine (M79.2)</content>. Average pain down to 3 of 10 and sleep better on duloxetine 60 mg, without drowsiness; part of the improvement may be the milder weather. Sodium 139 mmol/L and ALT 27 U/L, both normal. Blood pressure 132/82, a little above his earlier readings, which duloxetine can do; to be rechecked. PHQ-9 3. [S]<list><item>Continue duloxetine 60 mg daily and capsaicin cream as needed.</item><item>Recheck blood pressure at the next visit.</item><item>Review in about five weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain severity (0 to 10 numeric rating) and overall improvement</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Average 3 of 10, from 6 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects, especially drowsiness, dizziness and nausea</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Early nausea only; no drowsiness or dizziness</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Daily activities and participation: fine hand tasks in the cold, driving, flying</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Manages fine tasks outdoors with gloves; driving</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 3</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Sleep quality</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sleeping through most nights</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Before starting duloxetine and periodically on it</td><td>Completed today</td><td>132/82 mmHg, up from 126/78 before duloxetine; recheck at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium and liver enzyme (ALT) on duloxetine</td><td>Baseline, then once in the first weeks of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sodium 139 mmol/L and ALT 27 U/L. Protocol deviation: the test ordered for two to four weeks after starting duloxetine was not drawn, because no date was booked and it was missed at the preventive visit; it was drawn today, six weeks after starting. No harm resulted: both results are normal. Corrective action: book the blood test with a date when a medicine needing monitoring is started</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interactions: no tramadol or other serotonergic drugs; caution with oral NSAIDs; alcohol</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No new medicines; rare alcohol</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin of the fingers and cold protection</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Skin intact</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Average finger pain at or below 3 of 10, with fewer cold attacks, by four months. In progress; target 1928-06-09 [S]</item><item>Able to handle buttons, keys and a pen outdoors in the cold with protective gloves. Outcome by 1928-05-05: Managing with liner and heated gloves and hand warmers [S]</item><item>Any medicine used causes no daytime drowsiness that affects driving or flying. In progress; target 1928-06-09 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in about five weeks, with a blood pressure check. Next scheduled visit 1928-06-09: seventeen-week review: finger pain, the ankle sprain, and the risks of an Arctic air search flight. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
